ponent of the Direction Vector is Zero" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119842 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119842 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1045 "The Cartesian Equation of a Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120015 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "28cf8e1a-d07a-556d-a006-2d022a4740c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120506 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120506 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120506 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120506 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120506 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120506 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ba33a02-c52b-5c65-80c6-40fc93de31d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120506 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{0}=\\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z-0}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{0}=\\frac{y-1}{2}=-z,\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x+1}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=-1:$\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1+2t \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x+1}{0}$ part and instead write $x=-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-1}{2}=-z,\\,x=-1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120707 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120707 #edb/ref 1061 17885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120707 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "762cbf4d-a393-51df-98bb-da96c034848b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120707 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120708 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120708 #edb/ref 1061 17886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120708 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e305d64-a5b0-5e37-85c9-271d9bd1785b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120708 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120709 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120709 #edb/ref 1061 17887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120709 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15d988ae-4b19-5d9f-a7e2-fd6fd8c3f4c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120709 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120710 #edb/ref 1061 17888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120710 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1e9d9f49-2eb3-5745-9da4-70a87dddd9f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120710 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120728 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120728 #edb/ref 1061 18457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120728 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c618a49e-3866-523c-b010-33f9b474436e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120728 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120729 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120729 #edb/ref 1061 18458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120729 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "669d1115-ca36-5052-810d-cb802269d1fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120729 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121048 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we know that the vector equation of the line $l$ is given by\n![](images/92/92c5ed55727717320b9c128adf1a6d786666adb38cd6ba71d57917e60d661fd8.svg)\n\nand we want to rewrite it using Cartesian coordinates. In other words, we want to create an equation that relates $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ to each other.\n\nWe can do this using the following $3$ steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the parametric equations for the line.\n\nSubstituting ![](images/67/67b8360f90540ffb75d39717c375f0ef6e9a8a39cae5832c78957105e0d148b6.svg) into the equation of $l,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+6t \\\\ -2+5t \\\\ 5-t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3+6t \\\\ y &=-2+5t \\\\ z &=5-t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n\\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-3}{6} &=t \\\\ \\frac{y+2}{5} &=t \\\\ \\frac{z-5}{-1} &=t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for $t$ in each of the parametric equations.\n\n![](images/ee/eed0a210b121af73bc2ceab04b95def59239a742055284e706a1532b47edc60d.svg)\n\n**Step 3:** Equate the resulting expressions. Because the right-hand side of each equation is equal to $t,$ we can write this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} = t\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by omitting the \"$=t$\" on the right-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} \\,.\n$$\n\nAnd we are done! \n\nThis way of writing the equation of a line is called the **Cartesian equation** or **Cartesian canonical equation** (or simply **canonical equation**).\n\nIn general, the Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186121048 #edb/ref 1065 3245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121048 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4b2a904a-eda3-58e1-9b0f-e742454f48b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121048 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121055 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to write the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n![](images/9d/9d4a6d56170de2e0dbfa7e2a6f51905b19f9ada9fa1900aafe2672f54831be86.svg)\n\nUsing the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/0b/0bea89e098af6be643450df08d30b97c0c3d658075b0b891ab2c4c7f37077f42.svg)\n\nIf we solve for $t$ in the parametric equations of the line, we get\n\n![](images/f3/f399dae55ae8c720f7769fa78a42b30b2a7b7aab49ed1515d9c43d371f3a9f71.svg)\n\nOnly the first two equations have right-hand sides equal to $t,$ so we combine them but keep the third equation separate:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}, \\; z=3\n$$\n\nSo, it's as if we removed the $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ part in the original formula and replaced it with $z=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121055 #edb/ref 106